HANDLES IN THE NEW GEMDOS:

SFT is the system handle table (never mind the name).

FTAB {
	PD *f_own;		/* owner; 0 means free */
	OFD *f_ofd;		/* OFD this handle refers to */
}

/* macros for setting & checking the OFD field in SFT */

#define ofdisatty(ofd) (((long)(ofd)) & 1)
#define ofddevno(ofd) ((short)((((unsigned long)(ofd)) >> 1)))
#define ofdmaketty(ofd,devno) ((ofd) = (OFD *)(((devno) << 1) | 1))

As you can see, the ofd field, when odd, says "this is a tty."  The rest of
the field is the BIOS device number of it.  The first NUMSTD slots are BIOS
devices 0-5, and they're owned by rootpd so they never go away.  This way
we handle processes which Fread/Fwrite on handle -1, for instance,
expecting the console; they get sft[-(-1)] which is sft[1], which is
initalized to BIOS device 2.  Fforce(0,-1) has a different effect: a new
entry in SFT is created with ofdmaketty(sft[h].f_ofd,(-1)+3).

Fopen of CON: AUX: etc. do NOT return negative numbers any more.  They
return positive handle numbers, whose slots in sft are TTY entries.

OFD is a structure representing a read/write pointer on a file. Many
entries in SFT from many processes may point to a single OFD, so it has a
reference count and no owner.  It has read/write pointer info and the open
mode -- read only, shared/exclusive, etc.  It also has a pointer
to the DFD.

OFD {
	OFD *o_next;
	int o_use;
	int o_mode;
	(r/w pointer info)
	DFD *o_dfd;
};

DFD is a structure containing the information specific to a file on disk.
This includes its starting cluster number, length, last-mod date & time,
file attributes, and also the drive/clust of the directory it's in, and the
offset in that directory of this file's slot (format TBD) so you can write
the date & time etc. back.

DFD {
	DFD *dfd_next;
	int dfd_use;

    /* the next 4 items must be in same order as FCB. In memory,	*/
    /* date, time, and strtcl are in 8088 format, but fileln isn't.	*/
	int	dfd_time;
	int	dfd_date;
	int	dfd_strtcl;
	long	dfd_fileln;

	int	dfd_dev;	/* device this file's on */
	int	dfd_dircl;	/* starting cluster of this file's dir */
	int	dfd_dirrec;	/* recno this file's slot is in */
	int	dfd_diroffs;	/* offset into dirrec of file's slot */
	int	dfd_dirty;	/* flag: new strtcl, len, date, or time */
}
